ms work much better on  men than they do on women and on white  people than they do on people of color  my next example brings us to France  where back in 2009 the French Ministry  of Education started to automate the  matching process of students to  universities and this of course is a  process that's really prone for  automation however the problem was that  the way the matching happened and the  criteria that were being used for this  matching process were kept secret in the  u.s. these the use of these kind of  systems had been really successful in  increasing the happiness of university  students parents however in France it  started evoking criticism among students  because they wanted to know what was  actually behind the system what kind of  values were leading the matching and  based on the French Freedom of  Information Law they sued the French  ministry to open up the code and what  started was a legal side that went on  until 2017 and in 2017 they finally won  and Fanny note on the side what the  French ministry did was they printed out  the code and send it by a post to the  student union  thanks again to a lot of volunteers that  they found through github they were able  to read it ties it and what they found  out was that one of the key factors for  matching students to universities was  actually the proximity of the address to  the to the universities address and if  you know a little bit about the French  education system you might know that all  the elite universities actually located  in like fancy areas around the center of  Paris so this system was reproducing  social discrimination and here this is a  super simple rule-based matching  it wasn't the complexity of the  technology there's usually this  narrative of the black box algorithm  that we cannot understand because of its  technical complexity this was not the  issue the issue was actually the  narrative that was created around this  black box problem and a conscious  decision to keep the system secret due  to trade secrets  due to maybe worries that it might evoke  protests my last example brings us to  Australia where the Ministry of Social  Welfare started using a system to detect  discrepancies between income reports and  information they held on social welfare  money that was received by people when a  discrepancy was identified by the system  it sent out automated warning mails to  people and the idea behind using the  system was to increase the efficiency of  finding the people that were receiving  well were wrongly receiving welfare  money so this actually could be in the  interest of whole society you save tax  money and they were pretty successful in  ramping up the efficiency they sent out  200,000 of these warning letters within  a week something they would have needed  a whole year before and in the testing  phase 200 thousands of these warning  letters were sent out to people shortly  before Christmas stating well we like to  inform you that you owe the stage 16  thousand euro and what they found out  was 20,000 of these 200,000 emails that  were sent out were actually wrong  so people were asked to pay a lot of  money start paying tomorrow and they  were not informed that there was an  automated system behind it they were not  informed how they came to the decision  and they were not really offered any  forms of Appeal and the Ministry that  had used the system because of its  efficiency at the same time was not  prepared for this backlash and they  weren't aware that efficiency also means  that mistakes scale  and actually if you go on Twitter today  it's been three years since they started  using the systems and you look for the  hashtag not my depth or Robo debt you  can still find stories of people who  haven't found justice who are left alone  and where the burden of proof is moved  from the ministry to those people  affected by these mistakes what these  three cases show is like I said that the  thin line between a system that's